# Project: Jest JavaScript Unit Testing Framework

## Framework Structure
- Use Jest as the primary test runner for JavaScript/TypeScript projects
- Organize tests in __tests__ directories or alongside source files with .test.js/.spec.js suffix
- Use TypeScript with ts-jest for type-safe test implementations
- Configure Jest via jest.config.js or jest.config.ts for modularity

## Coding Standards
- Follow JavaScript/TypeScript best practices with ESLint integration
- Use meaningful, descriptive test and variable naming
- Implement clean code principles with single-responsibility tests
- Limit describe block nesting to 3 levels maximum for readability

## Test Organization
- Group related tests using describe blocks with clear context names
- Use it/test functions for individual test cases with action-oriented names
- Implement beforeEach/afterEach/beforeAll/afterAll for setup and teardown
- Separate unit, integration, and snapshot tests into distinct directories

## Best Practices
- Follow Arrange-Act-Assert (AAA) pattern for test clarity
- Write focused, single-responsibility tests covering one behavior
- Avoid test interdependencies and shared mutable state
- Use jest.fn() for mocking instead of manual mock implementations
- Prefer .mockReturnValue() and .mockResolvedValue() for clean mock setup

## Mocking Strategies
- Use jest.mock() for automatic module-level mocking
- Implement jest.spyOn() for method-level spying without full mocks
- Use jest.useFakeTimers() with modern timer mode for time-dependent tests
- Mock HTTP requests with jest-fetch-mock, msw, or nock
- Always call jest.clearAllMocks() or configure clearMocks: true in jest.config

## Assertions
- Use expect() with specific, descriptive matchers for clear failure messages
- Implement custom matchers via expect.extend() for domain-specific assertions
- Use toMatchSnapshot()/toMatchInlineSnapshot() for stable output testing
- Prefer toEqual for deep equality; use toBe only for primitives and references
- Assert on thrown errors with expect().toThrow() specifying message or error type

## Coverage
- Configure coverage with --coverage flag or collectCoverage: true in jest.config
- Use V8 or Babel coverage providers based on project setup
- Set meaningful thresholds (statements, branches, functions, lines) in jest.config
- Use coveragePathIgnorePatterns for generated files and vendor code
- Generate HTML and lcov reports for local visualization and CI integration

## Performance
- Use --runInBand for debugging; avoid it in CI for parallel performance
- Use testPathPattern and testNamePattern for targeted test runs
- Use --bail to stop after first failure in CI for faster feedback
- Configure maxWorkers to match CI runner CPU count
- Set --testTimeout for async tests to prevent hanging suites

## Async Testing
- Use async/await for asynchronous test cases
- Return promises from test functions when not using async/await
- Use jest.runAllTimers() or jest.advanceTimersByTime() with fake timers
- Implement done callback only for callback-style async code
- Always await async assertions to avoid false positives

## Snapshot Testing
- Keep snapshots small and focused on stable UI or data structures
- Review snapshot diffs carefully before committing updates
- Use toMatchInlineSnapshot() for small, readable inline snapshots
- Store snapshots in version control; update intentionally with --updateSnapshot
- Avoid snapshotting dynamic or time-dependent values

## Test Data Management
- Use factory functions or fixtures for reusable, maintainable test data
- Define TypeScript interfaces for all test data structures
- Avoid hardcoded magic values; use descriptive named constants
- Generate realistic test data with @faker-js/faker when variety matters

## CI/CD Integration
- Configure jest --ci flag for stricter behavior in pipelines
- Use --reporters=jest-junit for CI pipeline test result reporting
- Enable test caching with --cache for faster repeated runs
- Upload coverage to Codecov, SonarQube, or Coveralls via dedicated actions
- Use --forceExit to prevent hanging processes in CI environments

## Environment Configuration
- Use testEnvironment: 'node' for Node.js code; 'jsdom' for DOM-dependent tests
- Configure moduleNameMapper for path aliases and static asset mocks
- Handle environment variables with dotenv in setupFiles
- Use globalSetup/globalTeardown for expensive one-time initialization

## Error Handling
- Test both success and error code paths for complete branch coverage
- Assert on specific error messages, types, and properties
- Use expect().rejects for async rejection assertions
- Test boundary conditions and edge cases explicitly

## Documentation
- Document complex test scenarios with concise inline comments
- Maintain test helper and fixture documentation in a dedicated README
- Include examples for custom matchers and test utilities
- Document project-specific mock strategies and shared fixtures

Remember to leverage Jest's rich ecosystem including Testing Library, jest-extended matchers, and babel-jest for modern JavaScript transformation.
